## a b s t r a c t
In order to realize outstanding electrochemical performance in oxygen evolution reactions (OER), it is important to construct 3D hierarchical nanospheres consisting of 2D bimetal metal–organic framework (MOF) nanosheets. Based on high-throughput density-functional theory (DFT) calculations, we chose Ni and V as central ions and prepared Ni-V bimetal MOFs nanospheres (NiV-MNs) assembled from ultrathin 2D MOFs nanosheets through a simple one-step solvothermal method. So far, V-based ultrathin 2D MOFs have been firstly reported. Gradient experiments demonstrated that NiV-MNs shows the best catalytic activity when the amount of Ni is equal to that of V (denoted as $\mathsf{N i}_{1}\mathsf{V}_{1}$ -MNs). The $\mathsf{N i}_{1}\mathsf{V}_{1}$ -MNs can deliver a high current density of $50\mathrm{mA}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ at a low over-potential of $370\mathrm{mV}$ in alkaline condition even after 10000 s continuous catalytic testing. Coincidentally, the DFT calculations further confirmed that the $\Delta G$ of $\mathsf{N i}_{1}\mathsf{V}_{1}$ -MNs is much lower compared with $\mathsf{N i}_{1}\mathsf{V}_{2}$ -MNs and $\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{V}_{1}$ -MNs. Based upon the experimental results and DFT calculations, we propose that the better performance of $\mathsf{N i}_{1}\mathsf{V}_{1}$ -MNs should be attributed to the grater intrinsic activity of Ni and the coupling effect between Ni and V, as they are crucial for tuning the electrochemical activity.
