## 1. Introduction
Electrochemical water splitting has attracted much attention due to its high-efficiency energy conversions.[1] OER is an important process in such energy conversions, especially in metal-air batteries.[2,3] However, presently used catalysts for OER typically concentrate on noble metal-based materials such as commercial $\sf P t/C$ and $\mathsf{R u O}_{2}$ .[4,5] Their high costs hinder large-scale practical applications.[4–6] Furthermore, electrocatalytic oxygen evolution process usually requires a large overpotential to drive the reaction, which also decreases the energy conversion efficiency.[7] Accordingly, great effort has been devoted to synthesizing promising alternatives for OER. Particularly, Ni-based materials draw considerable attention because of their low cost and upper electrocatalysis activity,[8–10] including NiP/NiS,[11] $\mathsf{N i}_{3}\mathsf{S e}_{2}$ nanoforest/Ni foam,[12] $\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{P@C}/$ Graphene,[13] etc. Wang et al.[13] prefabricated $\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{P}@\mathsf{C}/$ Graphene with a remarkably low overpotential of $285~\mathrm{mV}$ at $10\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ . Liu et al.[14] reported an Ni-MOF nanosheet array on Ni foam, which drives $100\mathrm{\mA\cm}^{-2}$ at an overpotential of $320~\mathrm{mV}$ in $1.0\mathrm{~M~}$ KOH. However, those Ni-based materials didn’t show satisfying OER activity and their electrochemical performance degraded rapidly.[8–14] The main reason for their unsatisfactory properties is the high $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{e_{g}}}$ -orbits filling of Ni, which restricts the improvement of OER performance. That is, combining Ni with other transition metals possesses lower $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{e_{g}}}$ -orbits filling, which would be able to significantly enhance the activity of the Nicontaining bimetal materials.[15]
MOFs are constructed by coordinated bonds between the metal ions nodes and organic ligands.[16,17] They generally have large specific surface areas and plenty of nanopores.[18] Due to the special structure, MOFs have several advantages, such as readily accessible active sites, fast mass transport, and conveniently recycling. [19] Recently, MOFs have been developed in the twodimensional nanosheets due to various merits including more active sites, faster mass and charge transfer, etc.[10,14] As a result, MOFs have been increasingly applied in electrochemical catalysis, [20] such as MOFs arrays,[10] MOFs-derived layered double hydroxides (LDHs),[21] Ni/Fe MOFs nanosheets,[22] etc. Collectively, two-dimensional MOFs have been widely recognized as promising alternative to noble-metal based catalysts in electrocatalysis.
Here, inspired by molecular orbital principles and the increasing application of DFT calculations in catalysis,[14,15] highthroughput DFT calculations were applied to help us to choose the metal central ions for the 2D MOFs system. The calculations results showed that the combination of Ni and $\mathsf{V}$ as central ions is a promising strategy for synthesizing high-performance 2D MOFs-based catalysts for OER. Structured 3D hierarchical nanospheres assembled from 2D building blocks would retain their 2D structural advantages and further offer faster electrons transfer when compared with dispersed unassembled 2D nanosheets. Consequently, guided by the calculation’s results, the Ni-V bimetal nanospheres assembled from 2D Ni-V MOFs nanosheets were successfully prepared from the mixed solution of $\mathsf{N i}^{2+}$ , $\mathsf{V}^{2+}$ , and benzenedicarboxylic acid (BDC) through a simple one-step solvothermal method. To investigate the effect of metal ratios on the activity, we prepared $\mathsf{N i}_{1}\mathsf{V}_{1}$ -MNs $(\mathsf{N i};\mathsf{V}=1{:}1\cdot$ , $\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{V}_{1}$ -MNs (Ni: $\mathsf{V}=2{:}1$ ) and $\mathsf{N i}_{1}\mathsf{V}_{2}$ -MNs $(\mathrm{Ni}{:}\mathsf{V}=1{:}2^{\cdot}$ ) respectively. The electrochemical measurements confirmed that $\mathsf{N i}_{1}\mathsf{V}_{1}$ -MNs offer better performance than $\mathsf{N i}_{1}\mathsf{V}_{2}$ -MNs and $\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{V}_{1}$ -MNs, delivering a high current density of $50\ \mathrm{mA}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ at a low overpotential of $370~\mathrm{mV}$ . Besides, no decay of activity was detected even after 10000 s continuous catalytic testing under a constant potential. To research the activity efficiency, the mass activity is also discussed in detail. Furthermore, the DFT calculations identify the lower $\Delta G$ values of $\mathsf{N i}_{1}\mathsf{V}_{1}.$ -
MNs for OER. The results mean that the energetically preferred reaction processes, and the coupling effect between Ni and $\mathsf{V}$ are verified by X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) and molecular orbital theory.
## 2. Materials and methods
## 2.1. Materials
Benzenedicarboxylic acid (BDC) and Vanadium(II) chloride $\left(\mathsf{V C l}_{2}\right)$ were purchased from Macklin. Potassium hydroxide (KOH), Nickel chloride hexahydrate $(\mathsf{N i C l}_{2}{\cdot}6\mathsf{H}_{2}0)$ , ethyl alcohol (EtOH, $99.5\%$ and N,N-dimethylformamide (DMF, $99.5\%$ were purchased from Sinopharm Chemical Reagent. Nafion (5 wt%) were purchased from Sigma-Aldrich.
## 2.2. Synthesis of $N i_{1}V_{1}$ -MNs
First, $32~\mathrm{mL}$ of DMF, $2{\mathrm{~mL~}}$ of deionized water (DI water) and $2\mathrm{mL}$ of ethanol were added into a $100\mathrm{mL}$ PE tube. Then, $0.75\mathrm{mmol}$ of BDC was ultrasonic dispersed in above solution. Next, $0.375\ \mathrm{mmol}$ of ${\mathsf{N i C l}}_{2}{\cdot}6{\mathsf{H}}_{2}0$ and $0.375\ \mathrm{mmol}$ of $\mathsf{V C l}_{2}$ were added in as-prepared mixed solution. Finally, the obtained solution was transferred to a $100~\mathrm{{mL}}$ reaction kettle at $413\mathrm{~K~}$ for $^{48\mathrm{~h~}}$ The product were obtained by centrifugation and washed with DI water for three times, and vacuum freeze-dried for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ .
The syntheses of $\mathsf{N i}_{1}\mathsf{V}_{2}$ -MNs and $\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{V}_{1}$ -MNs are similar to that of $\mathsf{N i}_{1}\mathsf{V}_{1}$ -MNs except for keeping the ratio of $\mathsf{N i}/\mathsf{V}$ at 1:2 and 2:1, respectively.
## 2.3. Material characterization
The morphology was recorded by SEM (Hitachi, S-4800) and TEM (JEOL JEM-2010). The Cs-corrected TEM image was characterized by a Cs-corrected TEM (JEM-ARM300F). The thickness of sample was measured by AFM. The ordinary STEM image and HRTEM image were recorded by a field emission and high resolution transmission electron microscope (JEOL JEM-2010). The FTIR spectra were measured by a Nicolet 6700 (KBr) FTIR spectrometer. The crystal structures were defined by XRD (Bruke D8 Advance diffractometer, Cu Ka1). The XPS was conducted on a Thermo escalab 250Xi X-ray photoelectron spectrometer. The ICP-MS was measured by the Agilent 7500ce system. Surface area and pore volume were measured using BET analyzer (ASAP 2460, MICROMERITICS INSTRUMENT CORP).
## 2.4. Electrochemical evaluations
The WE was prepared by depositing the mixed solution of $0.2~\mathrm{mL}$ DI water, $0.3~\mathrm{mL}$ ethanol, $0.02\mathrm{\mL}5\mathrm{\wt\%}$ Nafion solution and $1.8~\mathrm{\mg}$ catalysts on the glassy carbon electrode (GCE, diameter $\mathbf{\sigma}=3.0\ \mathrm{mm}^{\cdot}$ ). To confirm the mass loading of $0.2~\mathrm{mg}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ , $0.4~\mathrm{mg}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ and $0.6~\mathrm{mg}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ , the used catalysts of above mixed solution were $4\upmu\mathrm{L},8\upmu\mathrm{L}$ and $12\upmu\mathrm{L}$ respectively. The electrochemical measurements were performed in a typical three-electrode system (Princeton PMC $1000\&500$ electrochemical workstation). A platinum $\left(\mathrm{Pt}\right)$ sheet and saturated calomel electrode were used as counter electrode (CE) and reference electrode (RE), respectively. All the tests were conducted in a polytetrafluoroethylene (PTFE) bottle with 1 M KOH (high-purity: $99.98\ \mathrm{\\%}$ solution $\left(\mathrm{pH}=13.4\right)$ . It should be noted that all the potentials measured were calibrated to the reversible hydrogen electrode (RHE) by the equation: $E_{\mathrm{RHE}}=E_{\mathrm{SCE}}+0.24+0.0592~\mathrm{pH}.$ . For electrode activation, potential cycling between $1.0\mathrm{V}$ and $1.7\mathrm{V}$ at the scan rate of $50\mathrm{mV}.\mathsf{s}^{-1}$ until the stable cycling voltammogram (CV) curves were obtained. The linear sweep voltammetry (LSV) curves were recorded at $5\mathrm{m}\mathrm{V}{\cdot}\boldsymbol{s}^{-1}$ to minimize the capacitive curren. The solution impedance of 1 M KOH solution was measured at ${\mathfrak{g}}\Omega$ . All the data was corrected with iR-compensation. The electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) was measured at open-circuit potential voltage in the frequency range from $0.01~\mathrm{Hz}$ to $10^{5}~\mathrm{Hz}$ The TOF was calculated by the formula below:
$$
\mathrm{TOF}=\frac{J\times A}{4\times F\times m}
$$
Here, $J$ is the current density $(\mathsf{A}\cdot\mathsf{c m}^{-2})$ at the given overpotential, and $A$ and $m$ are the area of WE and the number of moles of the catalysts respectively. $F$ is the Faraday constant (96485 $C{\cdot}\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$ ).
## 2.5. Calculation details
All the DFT calculation processes were based on the pseudopotential plane-wave (PPW) method with spin-polarized. Electronion interactions were described by the ultrasoft pseudopotentials. The valence configurations are $2s^{2}2\mathsf{p}^{6}3s^{2}3\mathsf{d}^{8}$ for Ni, $2s^{2}2\mathsf{p}^{6}3s^{2}3\mathsf{d}^{3}$ for V, $2s^{2}2\mathrm{p}^{4}$ for O, $2s^{2}2\mathrm{p}^{2}$ for C and $\textbf{1}s^{1}$ for H, $2s^{2}2\mathsf{p}^{6}$ for Mg, $2s^{2}2\mathrm{p}^{6}$ for Al, $2s^{2}2\mathsf{p}^{6}3s^{2}3\mathsf{d}^{1}$ for Sc, $2s^{2}2\mathsf{p}^{6}3s^{2}3\mathsf{d}^{5}$ for Mn, $2s^{2}2{\mathsf{p}}^{6}3s^{2}3{\mathsf{d}}^{10}$ for Cu, $2s^{2}2{\mathsf{p}}^{6}3s^{2}3{\mathsf{d}}^{10}$ for Zn and $2s^{2}2\mathsf{p}^{6}3s^{2}3\mathsf{d}^{5}$ for Cr. Considering e-e exchange and correlation interaction, the Perdew-Burke-Ernzerhof (PBE) functional, a kind of general gradient approximation (GGA), was applied with a plane-wave basis set at a cutoff kinetics energy of $380~\mathrm{eV}$ throughout the calculations.
To study the mechanistic of the electrochemical reactions, a vacuum slab of $20\mathring{\mathsf{A}}$ was used to confirm the periodical cells which were well separated. According to the generally observed (200) plane and the Cs-STEM image, we supposed the plane (200) was the most exposed surface to the solution. So the plane (200) was used to study the interaction between catalysts and adsorbates. Due to the fact that surface stability is reversely depended on the number of dandling bonds, the number of broken bonds were minimized to ensure the lower energy and higher stability of the exposed surface. In this work, all the calculations were in the condition of $U=0\vee$ (no input external electric work).
During the geometry, the bottom atoms were constrained at fixed position. The Brillouin-zone integrations were performed using the Monkhorst-Pack grids of $(1\times8\times4)$ k-point grids for cell optimization and surface cell, and $0_{2}$ and water molecules were calculated in a $10\times10\times10\mathrm{~}\mathring{\mathsf{A}}^{3}$ box with a Gamma-point-only kpoint grids. It should be noted that all the results were obtained based on the Ni sites due to the lower $\Delta G$ value of Ni. As for $\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{V}_{1}$ -MNs and $\mathsf{N i}_{1}\mathsf{V}_{2}$ -MNs, a $3\times3\times3$ supercell was built to fit the ratio of $\mathsf{N i}/\mathsf{V}$ . The convergence criteria for self-consistent energy was set to $10^{-4}~\mathrm{Ha}$ , the max force was set to $0.02\ \mathrm{H}\mathbf{a}{\cdot}\mathring{\mathbf{A}}^{-1}$ and max displacement was set to $0.001\mathrm{~\AA~}$
## 3. Results and discussions
MOFs are constructed through coordinate-covalent bonds created between central metal ions and organic ligands. When used for catalysis, the central metal ions play a major role during the reaction processes, as such the selection of the central metal ions is crucial for synthesizing high-performance catalysts. Accordingly, we performed high-throughput DFT calculations focusing on 2D bimetal MOFs that modeled different central metal ions combinations (the metal atoms were set to be arranged alternately so the metal ratio was kept to 1:1) with BDC ligand for OER. During the reaction processes, the lattice plane (200) were considered to be the most exposed surface to the solution,[14] which was confirmed by the X-ray diffraction (XRD). The general result of observed (200) plane in high-resolution transmission electron microscopy (HRTEM) image and ambient atmosphere spherical aberrationcorrected TEM (Cs-corrected TEM) results shown below, and all DFT calculations were performed based on the edge of the lattice plane (200). To obtain accurate calculation results, the magnetic states of the metal atoms were considered, and all the models were set to the most stable spin states (Figure S1 and Table S1). Meanwhile, OER is a multi-step reaction, during which four electrons transferred from $\mathsf{O H}^{-}$ , leading to the oxidation of $\mathrm{{OH^{-}}}$ to $0_{2}$ . We proposed that four electrons were transferred in sequence, and this was validated by the calculations and previous report.[14,22]
The high-throughput calculations results are shown in Fig. 1, with the $\Delta G$ for four electrons transfer steps shown respectively. Based upon the calculation’s results, we found that the $\mathsf{N i}/\mathsf{V}$ combination employed as central ions would be an ideal strategy. Compared with other transition metal combinations, $\mathsf{N i}/\mathsf{V}$ could offer lower $\Delta G$ to yield better performance for OER.[14] Although the $\Delta G$ of $\mathsf{N i}/\mathsf{V}\ (0.98\ \mathrm{eV})$ in step (i) was slightly higher than $\mathsf{N i}/\mathsf{S c}$ $(0.96\mathrm{eV})$ , the situation in step (ii) was quite in the opposite (higher $\Delta G$ of $\mathsf{N i}/\mathsf{S c}$ meaning larger overpotential). Overall, the theoretical overpotential of $\mathsf{N i}/\mathsf{S c}$ was obviously higher than $\mathsf{N i}/\mathsf{V}\left(1.71\ \mathrm{eV}>1\right.$ . $54\mathrm{eV},$ . Besides, Ni/Mn may also possess excellent activity since the theoretical overpotential is nearly equal to $\mathsf{N i}/\mathsf{V}$ $\cdot1.57\mathrm{~\eV~\approx~}$ $1.54\:\mathrm{eV}$ ), which should be attributed to the variable valence states for easy electrons transfer. As for Cu and Zn, the stable single valence state for the charge transfer was not desirable. In summary, the $\mathsf{N i}/\mathsf{V}$ combination could offer the best performance in our study combination systems. According to the calculations results, we finally chose the $\mathsf{N i}/\mathsf{V}$ as central metal ions to prepare the $\mathsf{N i}_{1}\mathsf{V}_{1}$ -MNs. More detailed discussions about $\mathsf{N i}_{1}\mathsf{V}_{1}$ -MNs are shown below.
The morphology and physical characterizations of $\mathsf{N i}_{1}\mathsf{V}_{1}$ -MNs are shown in Fig. 2. The hierarchical nanostructure with a diameter of several microns, in which the nanospheres are composed of many ultrathin nanosheet building blocks. It can be examined using scanning electron microscope (SEM) and transmission electron microscope (TEM) images (Fig. 2a and S2). Notably, the $\mathsf{N i}_{1}\mathsf{V}_{2}$ -MNs and $\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{V}_{1}$ -MNs also possess a spherical shape with similar size (Figure S3). The HRTEM image is shown in Fig. 2b. The clear lattice fringes demonstrate high crystallinity, which coincides with the fast Fourier-transform high-resolution transmission electron microscope (FFT-HRTEM) image (Figure S4). It also should be noted that the interplanar spacing of plane (200) matches well with the theoretical value $(0.996~\mathrm{{nm}})$ ). The atomic arrangements were further confirmed by the Cs-corrected TEM image under HAADF-STEM mode (Fig. 2c). The alternating light and dark dots representing different Ni and $\mathsf{V}$ metal atoms demonstrated that the Ni and V atoms are arranged alternately and bonded through Ni-O-V bonding. Notably, $\mathsf{N i}_{1}\mathsf{V}_{1}$ -MNs possess more Ni-O-V bonding due to the equal amount of Ni and V, which is crucial for enhancing the electrocatalysis activity of OER, and it will be discussed below. In addition, it also demonstrated that the metal atoms on the lattice plane (200) were distributed in a hexagonal form,[14] in which the distance between nearest and second nearest metal atoms are $1.9\mathring{\mathsf{A}}$ and $3.5\mathrm{~\AA},$ respectively. It was in agreement with the DFT calculated value ( $1.8\mathring{\mathsf{A}}$ and $3.4\mathring{\mathsf{A}}$ after geometric optimization) (Figure S5). To determine the thickness of the samples, $\mathsf{N i}_{1}\mathsf{V}_{1}$ - MNs samples were ultrasonically dispersed in ethanol for $^{2\mathrm{~h~}}$ to obtain a single nanosheet. The thickness measured by an atomic force microscope (AFM) was about $10\ \mathrm{nm}$ (Fig. 2d), which means only about 11 coordination layers (Figure S6) greatly benefited mass transport and charge transport during the OER process. [23,24] The hierarchical nanostructure can be more closely examined by scanning the transmission electron microscopy (STEM) image (Fig. 2e). It further confirmed that the three-dimensional nanosphere were obtained from the 2D ultrathin nanosheets selfassembly. Due to the ultrathin feature, the edge of the nanosheets spontaneously wrinkled upon drying (Figure S2).
Subsequent elemental composition analysis was carried out by energy dispersive spectroscopy (EDS), revealing the obtained $\mathsf{N i}_{1}\mathsf{V}_{1}$ -MNs which contain C, O, Ni, V (Figure S7). The associated mapping images (Fig. 2f and S8) indicate the uniform distribution of these elements in ${\mathsf{N i}}_{1}{\mathsf{V}}_{1}{\mathsf{-M N s}}$ . The EDS quantitative analysis (Figure S7) further confirms the elementary composition was close to the theoretical value without any impurities, which was further confirmed by XPS survey in (Fig. 3a), with no impurities were detected. Furthermore, the inductively coupled plasma mass spectrometry (ICP-MS) confirmed that the samples were composed of $16.4~\mathrm{wt\%}$ Ni and $15.7\mathrm{\wt{\%}\ V}$ for $\mathsf{N i}_{1}\mathsf{V}_{1}$ -MNs, $22.3~\mathrm{wt\%}$ Ni and 11.1 wt% V for $\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{V}_{1}$ -MNs, 11.3 wt% Ni and $20.7~\mathrm{wt\%}~\mathrm{V}$ for $\mathsf{N i}_{1}\mathsf{V}_{2}$ - MNs, respectively. The XRD patterns (Fig. 3b) demonstrate that NiV-MNs are isostructural to previously reported Ni-MOFs (Cambridge Crystallographic Data Center, No. 985792), and the peak positions agree well with the theoretical values.[14] The four diffraction peaks should be assigned to (200), (001), (201), $\left({-201}\right)$ lattice planes, respectively. More functional groups and chemical bond information were identified by the Fourier transform infrared spectroscopy (FTIR) results (Fig. 3c). The peaks at $725~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , $1278\ \mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , $1375~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , $1552~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , $1695~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ and $3440~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ are attributed to the $C{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{H}$ bending vibration, $_{0-\mathrm{H}}$ bending vibration, $\scriptstyle{\mathsf{C}}=0$ stretching vibration, ${\mathsf{C}}{\mathrm{-}}{\mathsf{C}}$ stretching vibration, $c{-}0$ stretching vibration and $0{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{H}$ stretching vibration, respectively. Notably, the peaks at $421~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ and $823~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ are the characteristic peaks of the Ni-V-MOFs, which are ascribed to Ni-O vibration and V-O vibration and can be identified by the FTIR spectrum comparison of BDC ligands and $\mathsf{N i}_{1}\mathsf{V}_{1}$ -MNs (Figure S9). [25,26] According to the above structural characterizations, the atomic arrangements of $\mathsf{N i}_{1}\mathsf{V}_{1}$ -MNs could be defined clearly. As shown in (Fig. 3d), in this structure, both the V and Ni are octahedrally coordinated with six O atoms in two ways: (i) four O atoms from carboxylates and two O atoms form hydroxyls. (ii) four O atoms from hydroxyls and two O atoms form carboxylates (Figure S10). As expected, this structure is consistent with the model structure in high-throughput calculations. Due to the termination of metal-O coordination bonding with the surface metal atoms, abundant coordinative unsaturated active metal sites were expected (Figure S11) on the exposed surface, which is crucial to improve the activity of OER.
In addition, the ${\sf N}_{2}$ adsorption–desorption isotherms of the synthesized Ni-V-MOFs were obtained at 77 K in liquid nitrogen with prior degassing the sample at $120^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ temperature for $^{6\mathrm{~h~}}$ . The isotherms and pore size distribution were shown in Figure S16, which is the characteristic feature of mesoporous materials. The illustrations of the BET surface area, pore volume and pore size of all the composites were shown in Table S2. The BET surface area of $\mathsf{N i}_{1}\mathsf{V}_{2}.$ - MNs was found to be $162.8144~\mathrm{m}^{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ . The pore volume and average pore size obtained from the nitrogen adsorption–desorption isotherm were $0.14814~\mathrm{cm}^{3}{\cdot}\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ and $3.6395~\mathrm{nm}$ respectively.
In order to evaluate the OER Performance of NiV-MNs, electrochemical measurements were conducted with a Princeton PMC $1000~\&~500$ electrochemical workstation in 1 M KOH solution. The working electrode (WE) was prepared by depositing asprepared samples on the glass-carbon electrode (GCE) (a mass loading of $0.2~\mathrm{mg}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ unless otherwise stated). Linear sweep voltammetry (LSV) tests were carried out to obtain the polarization curves. As shown in (Fig. 4a), the $\mathsf{N i}_{1}\mathsf{V}_{1}$ -MNs can reach the current density of $50\mathrm{mA}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ at a much lower overpotential (the thermodynamic OER potential $\left(\mathrm{E}^{0}\mathrm{H}_{2}0/0_{2}=1.23\mathrm{V}\right)$ was used as reference) at $0.37~\mathrm{mV}$ compared with $\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{V}_{1}$ -MNs $(460~\mathrm{mV})$ and $\mathsf{N i}_{1}\mathsf{V}_{2}$ -MNs $(490\mathrm{mV})$ ). Notably, the different peaks between the potential range from $1.3\mathrm{V}$ to $1.4\mathrm{V}$ and the reversible hydrogen electrode (RHE) are attribute to the oxidation of Ni.[12] The Tafel plots are shown in Fig. 4b, indicating that $\mathsf{N i}_{1}\mathsf{V}_{1}$ -MNs possess a much lower Tafel slope $(42\ \mathrm{mV}.\mathrm{dec}^{-1}$ ) than those of $\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{V}_{1}$ -MNs $62\ \mathrm{mV}.\mathrm{dec}^{-1},$ ) and $\mathsf{N i}_{1}\mathsf{V}_{2}$ -MNs $(87\mathrm{~mV}.\mathrm{dec}^{-1},$ ). The smaller Tafel slope of $\mathsf{N i}_{1}\mathsf{V}_{1}$ -MNs suggests preferable reaction kinetics.[27] Besides the Tafel slope, cycling stability is another key parameter to evaluate performance.[28] As can be seen from (Fig. 4c), the current density of $\mathsf{N i}_{1}\mathsf{V}_{1}$ -MNs remains almost unchanged at an overpotential of $380\mathrm{mV}$ for $10000s.$ What’s more, the LSV curves also demonstrate that no obvious degradation appeared after a 10000 s chronoamperometric test. Furthermore, the XRD pattern shows no obvious change after OER (Figure S12), which is also confirmed by the SEM image after OER (Figure S13) and further implies the excellent crystal structural stability. The outstanding electrocatalytic activity of $\mathsf{N i}_{1}\mathsf{V}_{1}$ -MNs $\cdot511.17\mathrm{h}^{-1}$ ) was also identified by the turnover frequency (TOF) at the overpotential of $370~\mathrm{mV}$ (Fig. 4d), which is about four times of $\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{V}_{1}$ -MNs $(127.79\ \mathrm{\h}^{-1})_{.}$ ) and $\mathsf{N i}_{1}\mathsf{V}_{2}$ -MNs $(117.12\ \mathrm{h}^{-1}\cdot$ ). Additionally, the higher TOF means a faster reaction rate, verifying superior OER performance of $\mathsf{N i}_{1}\mathsf{V}_{1}$ -MNs. Meanwhile, it also should be noted that these samples show similar electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) results, showing that the conductivity is not responsible for the activity difference. In other words, the coupling effect between Ni and $\mathsf{V}$ and better intrinsic activity of $\mathsf{N i}$ are the reasons for the higher activity (Figure S14).
As catalyst for OER, mass activity is also a crucial factor to assess the performance.[29] Less mass loading with higher current density means better activity and lower cost for industrial production.[30] As shown in (Fig. 5a), $\mathsf{N i}_{1}\mathsf{V}_{1}$ -MNs have much better mass activity than $\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{V}_{1}$ -MNs and $\mathsf{N i}_{1}\mathsf{V}_{2}$ -MNs.
The potential required to achieve $100\ \mathsf{A}{\cdot}\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ is only $1.56~\mathrm{V}$ which is much lower than other samples in this work. Moreover, there is no obvious change in mass activity even after being stored under air atmosphere for one month (Fig. 5b), clarifying excellent stability and satisfying activity. To further explore the mass activity, electrochemical measurements of $\mathsf{N i}_{1}\mathsf{V}_{1}$ -MNs at different mass loadings were performed. As shown in (Fig. 5c), as the mass loading increased, the current density also increased, meaning more catalyst would benefit and accelerate the reaction rate. However, this increase doesn’t mean an intrinsic enhancement of activity. (Fig. 5d) illustrates the TOF values of different mass loadings at various potentials. Notably, the TOF values reach the highest when the mass loading is $0.2\ \mathrm{\mg}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ , not the higher mass loading of $0.4{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ and $0.6~\mathrm{mg}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ , impling that increasing the amount of the catalyst is not a ideal choice when the cost is taken into consideration, and less mass loadings can still achieve relatively high TOF for $\mathsf{N i}_{1}\mathsf{V}_{1}$ -MNs. In summary, these electrochemical results clarify the coupling effect between $\mathsf{N i}$ and $\mathsf{v}$ is responsible for the high activity of $\mathsf{N i}_{1}\mathsf{V}_{1}$ -MNs and Ni shows better intrinsic activity.
In order to investigate the coupling effect between the metal sites in NiV-MNs, high-resolution XPS of $\mathsf{N i}_{1}\mathsf{V}_{1}$ -MNs, $\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{V}_{1}$ -MNs and $\mathsf{N i}_{1}\mathsf{V}_{2}$ -MNs were conducted respectively.[33] To obtain credible XPS results, the possible effect of background line setting was ruled out by applying uniform background line setting for the same elements. As shown in Fig. 7, the peak of $\ensuremath{\mathsf{N i}}_{2\ensuremath{\mathbf{p}}}$ in $\mathsf{N i}_{1}\mathsf{V}_{1}$ -MNs appeared at higher binding energy compared with $\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{V}_{1}$ -MNs and $\mathsf{N i}_{1}\mathsf{V}_{2}$ -MNs (Fig. 7a, 7b and 7c). Conversely, the peak of $\mathsf{V}_{2\mathrm{p}}$ in $\mathsf{N i}_{1}\mathsf{V}_{1}$ -MNs appeared at lower binding energy (Fig. 7d, 7e and 7f). This means that more electrons transferred from Ni sites to V sites through the Ni-O-V bonding, which significantly enhance the valence of Ni. It has been reported that such process is crucial to promote the activity for OER.[34,35] Furthermore, this charge transfer process could be explained by their electronic configurations. The valence electronic configuration of $\mathsf{V}^{2+}$ is ${3\mathsf{d}}^{3}$ . Therefore, $\mathsf{V}^{2+}$ can interact with the $0^{2-}$ through $\pi$ -donation. As for $\mathsf{N i}^{2+}$ , the $\mathbf{t}_{2\mathbf{g}}$ -orbits are fully occupied with a valence electronic configuration of $\mathrm{\Delta}3\mathrm{d}^{8}$ , as a result, $\mathbf{e^{-}}\mathbf{\lambda}\mathbf{e^{-}}$ repulsion is the dominating interaction between $0^{2-}$ and $\mathsf{N i}^{2+}$ (Fig. $\mathbf{7g}\mathbf{\dot{\mathrm{.}}}$ ). As such, the $\pi$ -donation between $0^{2-}$ and $\mathsf{V}^{2+}$ is strengthened by the repulsion between $0^{2-}$ and $\mathsf{N i}^{2+}$ , which facilitates partial charge transfer from $\mathsf{N i}^{2+}$ to $\mathsf{V}^{2+}$ through the Ni-O-V bonding. When it comes to the $\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{V}_{1}$ -MNs and ${\mathsf{N i}}_{1}{\mathsf{V}}_{2}{\mathsf{-M N s}}$ , these interactions are not as strong as $\mathsf{N i}_{1}\mathsf{V}_{1}.$ MNs due to less Ni-O-V bonding (Fig. 7h, 7i and S15), consequently, they deliver lower catalytic activity. These experimental and theoretical results indicate that the using interaction between different metal atoms is a promising way to develop various excellent catalysts for OER.
## 4. Conclusion
In conclusion, based upon the high-throughput DFT calculations, Ni-V bimetal MOFs nanospheres assembled from associated 2D MOFs nanosheets were successfully prepared through a simple one-step solvothermal method and then used as electrocatalysts for OER in alkaline condition. Interestingly, the better performance of $\mathsf{N i}_{1}\mathsf{V}_{1}$ -MN is due to the intrinsic activity of Ni and the coupling effect between Ni and $\mathsf{V}$ according to the DFT calculation and XPS survey, which is also essential to tune the electrochemical activity. Remarkably, the Ni-V MOFs nanospheres show the best activity when the amount of Ni is equal to V so that the $\mathsf{N i}_{1}\mathsf{V}_{1}$ -MNs can deliver a high current density of $50\mathrm{mA}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ at a low overpotential of $370\mathrm{mV}$ in alkaline condition even after 10000 s continuous catalytic testing. This work will not only provide new views to understand the structure–activity relationship but also offer a practical strategy for synthesizing a series of catalysts for OER with atomic precision.
